@page "/admin/revenue"
@layout NFHotel.Web.Components.Layout.AdminLayout
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = StaffPolicies.SalesView)]

@*
    An honest placeholder — no ViewModel, no service, no figures.

    Architecture.md section 7, point 5: BR-31 always set revenue to 0, and there is
    no price in the domain. A SalesService that returned 0 would look like an implementation,
    and the old system already had an empty SalesOverviewViewModel. The screen therefore says
    what is missing instead of showing a false zero.

    No @rendermode: the page is static text and has nothing to be interactive about.
*@

<PageTitle>Revenue — administration</PageTitle>

<div class="stack-lg">
    <PageHeader Title="Revenue"
                Subtitle="The screen is here, but the numbers can't be worked out yet." />

    <AlertBox Kind="AlertKind.Info">
        <p>
            <strong>The system has no pricing model.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
            Rooms, bookings and services carry no price, and nothing records payments. Any
            total would come out as 0 — and a zero that looks like a real number is worse
            than no screen at all.
        </p>
    </AlertBox>

    <section class="card card-pad stack" aria-labelledby="why-not-roomsize">
        <h2 class="subtitle" id="why-not-roomsize">Why <code>RoomSize</code> isn't enough</h2>

        <p>
            A room has a size — single, double or suite — but that is a <em>category</em>,
            not a price. It says how many people can sleep in the room, not what the night
            costs. Multiplying a category by a number of nights would be a guess dressed up
            as accounting.
        </p>

        <p>
            A price also depends on more than size: season, day of the week, corporate
            agreements and whatever was sold on top. None of that exists in the domain today.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card card-pad stack" aria-labelledby="what-is-needed">
        <h2 class="subtitle" id="what-is-needed">What has to come first</h2>

        <ol class="stack-sm">
            <li>
                A pricing model in the domain. Price per room size, per night or per season
                is a business decision, not a technical one.
            </li>
            <li>
                A notion of payment, so "confirmed" (payment guaranteed) and "paid" can be
                told apart.
            </li>
            <li>
                Only then an <code>ISalesService</code> with real use cases — and a screen
                that shows them.
            </li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <p class="muted">
        Until then, the <a href="/admin/bookings">booking list</a> shows how full the hotel
        is, and the <a href="/admin">dashboard</a> shows today's movements.
    </p>
</div>
